spec:
  grid: 16x16, background orange(7), single foreground color gray(5)
  bars:
    description: 1-pixel-thick diagonal line segments; two orientations NW-SE (\) and NE-SW (/)
    total_count: 2-6
    both_orientations_required: true, each orientation has >=1 bar
    length_per_bar: 2-10 pixels
    pixel_counts_unequal: total pixels in one orientation must differ from the other
  adjacency:
    no_4_connected: no two fg pixels share a horizontal or vertical edge
    unambiguous_orientation: every fg pixel has diagonal neighbors in exactly one orientation (XOR)
    min_chebyshev_between_orientations: 2 (bars of different orientation never touch diagonally)
    min_chebyshev_same_orientation: >=2 between distinct bars of same orientation
  density: fg pixels 5-29 out of 256, roughly 2-11% [observed]
  output:
    same_grid_size: true
    background_unchanged: true
    larger_orientation_group: recolored teal(8)
    smaller_orientation_group: recolored red(2)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction with separation guarantees
  steps:
    1. Create 16x16 grid filled with orange(7).
    2. Choose total number of bars (2-6), split between NW-SE and NE-SW (each >=1).
    3. For each bar, pick a random length (2-10) and a starting cell; extend diagonally.
       Place bars one at a time; after each placement, verify the new bar has
       Chebyshev distance >=2 from all existing bars of the opposite orientation
       and >=2 from bars of the same orientation. Retry placement if violated.
    4. After all bars placed, verify total pixel counts differ between orientations.
       If equal, extend or shorten one bar by 1 pixel (or regenerate).
    5. Assign gray(5) to all bar pixels.
  output_derivation: classify each pixel by diagonal-neighbor orientation;
    recolor the larger group teal(8) and the smaller group red(2).
```
